bitsim: run an iCE40 UP5K bitstream directly, two-valued, cycle exact against the gate cosim.

  iceunpack X.bin X.asc
  make ASC=X.asc PCF=vera_module.pcf O=build     (PROFILE= optional, see below)
  build/replay EV OUT --clk50 PS [--mem PREFIX Z0,Z1,Z2,Z3]

--mem loads PREFIX0..3 (the RTL banks blk0..3) into SPRAM cells Z0..Z3. Placement decides which cell holds
which bank (s7 3,2,4,1, nb1 2,3,1,4, casc_s4 3,1,4,2). make writes it to build/memorder: memmap writes one
byte into each bank over the bus and sees which cell it lands in. A wrong order looks like real video
mismatches. tgen.py takes the same string as --mem-order.

asc2net.py   .asc -> flat netlist, routing collapsed
checknet.py  X.net routed.json: every cell at its BEL, config and nets, must equal the routed design
gen.py       netlist -> bitsim_gen.c + bitsim_pins.h, straight-line levelized C
runtime.inc  BRAM, SPRAM, MAC16 models (config arguments fold at compile time)
bitsim.h     API: bitsim_reset/set/release/eval/get/driven/contention/spram_load
replay.c     replays a recorded bench and compares every output pad sample
mkbench.py   tb_scene_t.v / tb_audio.v -> gate-only recording bench

Netlist lines (net 0/1 constants, -1 not connected):
  lut OUT I0 I1 I2 I3 INITHEX X Y N        carry COUT I1 I2 CIN X Y N
  ff Q D CLK CEN SR NEG SET ASYNC X Y N    alias A B
  io NAME TYPE NEG PAD DIN0 DIN1 D0 D1 OE CEN ICLK OCLK LATCH X Y Z     refpad NAME
  ram X Y RMODE WMODE NEGR NEGW RCLK RCLKE RE WCLK WCLKE WE RADDR*11 WADDR*11 MASK*16 WDATA*16 RDATA*16 INITHEX
  spram X Y Z pins      mac X Y Z NEG_TRIGGER=n CFG=n... : pins      pll OUTNET LOCKNET ...

Evaluation: settle, find clock edges, then for all domains that fired, in order: memory and MAC
reads, memory writes, FF loads, RDATA commits. Repeat until no edge, up to DELTA_MAX; non-convergence
returns -1. Same semantics as nonblocking assigns in the gate netlist, derived clocks a delta late.
A combinational loop becomes a bounded inner loop and gen.py reports it (none in VERA).
clk50 and pll_lock are pins, the PLL is not modelled; clk25 connects to nothing else.

Speed comes from running only what can change: partitions with dirty flags, FF groups and memory
ports with an input-moved flag, MAC/RAM outputs unpacked only when the word changed. LUTs in one
partition with the same table, some inputs shared (selects) and the rest one net per lane, form a
bank: its lane operands and outputs are numbered contiguously in v[] and evaluated 32 lanes per AVX2
op (--vec-min 16; the VRAM read muxes in VERA, ~3% in x16emu; --no-vec turns it off). Without a profile,
partitions are 16 nodes in level order. PROFILE="EV OUT --clk50 PS" first replays any trace on this
image, recording which nets change in each eval (trig.txt), and gen.py --trig grows each partition
with the ready node that adds the fewest new wake-ups on those records, closing it when running the
node alone would cost less (--trig-call: one partition run in node evaluations, 8; --trig-fixed: the
D test every partition costs on each eval, 0.1; together ~160 partitions on s7). On s7 this halves
the LUT evaluations and runs ~1.3x faster than the older activity order (ACT=act.txt, --act); an s7
profile carries over to the other traces. Results do not depend on it, speed does: net numbers are per
image, so never reuse a profile from another image. The emulator's own workload makes a better profile than
a trace: `make emu-count` builds $(O)/ecount/emu/x16emu, which writes trig.txt (every 1024th eval) where
it runs; then `make emu TRIG=.../trig.txt`. Trained on 3 s of VERATESTALL, the boot screen ran ~15%
faster than with the s7 trace profile.

Not modelled, gen.py stops: DDR and registered or latched IO inputs, PLL types other than PAD,
dynamic SPRAM SLEEP/POWEROFF. WARMBOOT ignored. MAC16 cascade pins read 0 (not in the chipdb).

x16emu-bitsim: a copy of x16emu with $9F20-$9F3F going to the image as bus cycles, so X16 programs
run against a bitstream with no board.

  make emu ASC=X.asc PCF=vera_module.pcf O=DIR           -> DIR/emu/x16emu
  SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy SDL_AUDIODRIVER=dummy DIR/emu/x16emu -c816 -rom ROM -prg X.PRG -run -warp \
      -fsroot DIR -echo -bitsim-secs N -bitsim-ppm screen.ppm

-bitsim-secs stops after N simulated seconds and prints the bus counts. -bitsim-ppm rewrites a screen
dump every simulated second. All the new code is src/vera_bus.c, the rest is #ifdef BITSIM. CPU clock
is VBUS_CPU_HZ, default 7170000 (OtterX, T80 reads 7.17 MHz; add -DVBUS_CPU_HZ=8000000 for an X16).

Every picosecond of VERA is simulated: each CPU cycle advances the pins by 1/VBUS_CPU_HZ whether or
not it touches VERA. clk50 is free running, so the CPU and VERA clocks drift against each other as
they do on the board. About 1/28 of real time on an idle i5-13400F P-core (27-28 s per simulated
second, boot and READY screen; built with an emu-count profile, see above); E-cores run ~1.5x slower.

Bus, from OtterX sheet BusDecoder. ~VERA_CS is A15-A5 decoded and nothing else (no PHI2, VDA, VPA or
bank), ~RE = PHI2 & RWB, ~WE = PHI2 & !RWB. So any cycle with its address in the VERA page is an
access, internal cycles included. The model plays these per cycle, times from PHI2 fall at the start
of the cycle (T = CPU period):

  +20 ns          extbus_a          tAH >= 10, tADS <= 30 (W65C816S)
  +30 ns          extbus_cs_n low   through the '138
  T/2 + 8 ns      rd_n or wr_n low  through the '00
  T/2 + 30 ns     write data on     tMDS
  T - 10 ns       read data taken   tDSR
  T + 8 ns        strobe high
  T + 15 ns       write data off    tDHW >= 10
  next cycle +30  cs_n high, if that cycle leaves the page

rd_n and wr_n only move on VERA cycles: outside the page they would pulse every cycle, but all they
feed is gated with cs_n.

The CPU core runs whole instructions and skips cycles that don't transfer data, so vera_bus.c lists
each instruction's cycles first (W65C816S table 5-7) and plays them. That gives the ghost cycles the
hardware sees: an indexed store's read of the un-carried target, a page-crossing read's, and the
extra cycle of a read-modify-write (read in native mode, a write of the old byte in emulation mode).
16-bit RMW writes go high byte first, as the chip does, not in the core's order. The core's cycle
count stands if it is longer (slow I/O), the list's if the core misses a penalty; -bitsim-secs prints
the opcodes where they differ. An access the list did not predict still happens and counts as stray.

The 65C02 has no list, it gets its data accesses only, one per call and no ghosts. Use -c816.
Addresses are 16 bits, like the decoder, so a long access to bank N, $9F2x reaches VERA here too;
with -gs banks above 0 go to RAM first. IRQ is sampled at the end of each instruction, not on the
penultimate cycle. HostFS takes no simulated time. spi_miso is held high (no card), SD does not work,
audio pads are ignored. The screen comes from the VGA pads at 640x480 timing; NTSC and RGB modes don't
map. x16emu's own VERA stays idle, so the debugger's VERA panes show nothing real.

VERATESTALL TAB counts 88D5 IRQs here and on stock x16emu, 8898 on the OtterX: x16emu's CPU/VIA timing,
not bitsim (every other line, CRCs included, matches the board).

tmnet.py: routed delays out of an image, from icetime's timing netlist and timings_up5k.txt (min/typ/max).

  make build/icetime        # icetime.patch: stock icetime leaves IO OUTPUTENABLE and the DSP and SPRAM
                            # clocks unconnected in its netlist, so those nets would get no delay
  build/icetime -d up5k -P sg48 -p x.pcf -o X.tv X.asc
  python3 tmnet.py X.asc X.tv [--corner 0|1|2] [--pads REGEX]     pad to FF, pad to pad, strobes to clock pins
  python3 tmnet.py X.asc X.tv --spi                               spi_sck and spi_mosi at the pads

tgen.py: the same netlist as Verilog with those delays on every cell input, for iverilog against the
reference traces, checked column by column like replay.

Every element carries its routed delay to each input pin plus its own arcs, rise and fall apart: LUTs
(route + arc to lcout or ltout, cascades included), carries, FFs (setup on D/ce/sr, clk to q, sr to q),
block RAM and SPRAM (setups, clock to each data bit), MAC16, IO (pad and PRE_IO arcs, output registers,
output enables), global buffers. Approximations: a setup is a delay on the input, so hold is not checked
separately; the PLL has no arcs in timings_up5k.txt (*), its output is the ideal clk50; a MAC16 output
fed by a register (OUTPUT_SELECT 1, or 2/3 with the 8x8/16x16 pipeline reg) uses the ALL_PIPELINE clock to
O arcs, its inputs the worst BYPASS input to O arc as setup, a combinational one the worst arc into each O.

  python3 tgen.py X.net X.tv OUT.v [--corner 0|1|2] [--scale 0]
  iverilog -g2012 -s tb -o X.vvp OUT.v cells_sim.v
  vvp -n X.vvp +ev=EV +out=OUT +clk50=PS [+mem=PREFIX] [+stop=PS] [+edge=PS]

--scale 0 drops every delay (the control, run it at +edge=300). A reference sample shows the state after
the last clk50 rise, at a phase that differs per trace, so a timed run samples +edge=17000 (default) after
that rise, past the clock to pad delays and before the next edge's. Read data is checked where the CPU
takes it: the pads as the read strobe ends against the reference's last value in that strobe (the design
can change it mid-strobe, with delays that reaches the pads later), which makes it a setup check for the
bus too. Slow: a few microseconds of chip time per second.

Results, s7 and casc_s4, a3/s4/s5/s7/f9 to 2 ms each: zero delay and typ match everywhere. At max, f9
differs in two ways, both timing and not model errors: a DC_VIDEO write shows one clk later (strobe to clk
synchronizer), and 6 of 1204 DATA0 reads come back stale (7 on casc_s4; same with the patched icetime's
DSP/SPRAM clock and IO enable delays). Those are the third of three DATA0 reads whose
strobes end 121 to 132 ns apart, about 3 clk; at max the fetch needs one clk more. A 65C02 at 12 MHz can't
read that fast (LDA abs is 4 cycles, 333 ns).

make test: runtime.inc's MAC16 and block RAM against yosys' cells_sim.v on random vectors, async reset pulses
included, every MAC configuration in the image plus random ones, all 16 RAM mode pairs. SPRAM is not covered:
cells_sim gives x on a write where the model holds, that assumption rests on the runs against hardware.
